WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:04.090
And we are now ready to prove Euler's 
theorem, that will

00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:10.270
help us to find paths visiting every
edge in the graph exactly once.

00:00:10.270 --> 00:00:14.980
Actually, we will be focusing on the
Eulerian Cycle problem.

00:00:14.980 --> 00:00:20.140
Find a cycle that visits every edge in the
graph, because Euler was interested in

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:25.990
a walk in Kernigsberg that starts and
ends at the same place.

00:00:25.990 --> 00:00:28.050
There is cosmetic, very small differences,

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.910
between finding Eulerian cycles and
Eulerian paths.

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:33.050
As soon as we learn how to find cycles,

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:36.660
we will learn how to find paths, and vice
versa.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.090
Let us start from a question.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.210
Does this graph have an Eulerian cycle?

00:00:41.210 --> 00:00:44.170
Or, in other words, is this graph
Eulerian?

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:50.860
Of course not, because there is no way out
of the red node.

00:00:50.860 --> 00:00:55.940
What about this graph?
It's also non Eulerian because there's no

00:00:55.940 --> 00:01:02.340
way in, into the the red node.
And what about this graph?

00:01:02.340 --> 00:01:06.440
We need to look a little bit more closer
but we will, immediately find

00:01:06.440 --> 00:01:12.750
a red node here for which there is one
incoming and two outgoing edges.

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.640
Which means that this graph cannot
possibly be Eulerian,

00:01:15.640 --> 00:01:21.440
because in every Eulerian cycle, the
number of times we enter into

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:25.120
a node equals to the number of times we
leave the node.

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:28.960
And therefore, the number of incoming
edges in every node should

00:01:28.960 --> 00:01:33.270
be equal to the number of outgoing edges
from every node.

00:01:33.270 --> 00:01:39.830
We call a graph balanced if, in-degree of
every node, equal to its out-degree.

00:01:39.830 --> 00:01:40.720
Or in other words, the number

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:44.930
of incoming edges to every node equals the
number of outgoing edges.

00:01:46.230 --> 00:01:48.730
Is this graph balanced?

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:50.970
Of course, we can see that for every

00:01:50.970 --> 00:01:54.620
node there are two incoming and two
outgoing edges.

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:57.900
But, it doesn't help us yet to figure out

00:01:57.900 --> 00:02:01.010
whether there is an Eulerian cycle in this
graph.

00:02:01.010 --> 00:02:05.670
And now, we are ready to present Euler's
theorem.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:09.680
We already saw that every Eulerian graph
is balanced.

00:02:10.680 --> 00:02:14.150
So this graph has a chance to be Eulerian.

00:02:14.150 --> 00:02:20.230
Euler proved that every balanced graph is
Eulerian, of course balanced

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:25.211
and strongly connected because if graph
is disconnected there's no way to find.

00:02:25.211 --> 00:02:30.910
Let's prove this theorem.
And to prove the theorem, we need to

00:02:30.910 --> 00:02:36.200
recruit an ant, and let it randomly walk
through the graph, of

00:02:36.200 --> 00:02:41.350
course with the condition that the ant
cannot visit the same edge twice.

00:02:42.390 --> 00:02:43.870
Let's start.

00:02:43.870 --> 00:02:48.829
If ant was a genius, then he would simply
walk along the graph.

00:02:50.150 --> 00:02:56.920
As I show here, visited all edges of the
graph, and finally,

00:02:56.920 --> 00:02:57.770
returned back.

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:04.330
But an average ant, wouldn't be that
lucky, and it would start travelling

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:08.990
through the graph, start walking, and
eventually, it would get stuck.

00:03:10.540 --> 00:03:12.800
Let's stop for a second and think.

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:16.940
But to what node this ant can possibly get
stuck?

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.200
Can it get stuck in the middle of the
graph?

00:03:20.790 --> 00:03:22.490
Presumably not,

00:03:22.490 --> 00:03:28.640
because our graph is balanced, and
therefore every time the ant is

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:32.740
entering into a node, there should be a
way out of the node.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:37.710
This condition holds for all nodes in the
graph, except for one.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:38.610
Which one?

00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:42.670
Of course, it's the starting node of the
ant, because in

00:03:42.670 --> 00:03:47.660
the starting node, the ant has already
used the first outgoing edge,

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:53.480
so it may arrive in this node and there
will be no out going edge left.

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:56.400
And therefore what we learn that the ant
can

00:03:56.400 --> 00:04:00.520
only get stuck at the node where it
starts.

00:04:01.960 --> 00:04:03.610
What can we do?

00:04:03.610 --> 00:04:10.330
Afterwards the ant failed to construct the
Eulerian Cycle, because it did not visit.

00:04:10.330 --> 00:04:12.400
all edges of the graph.

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:13.960
But maybe,

00:04:13.960 --> 00:04:18.670
just maybe, we can start at a different
node in the green cycle.

00:04:18.670 --> 00:04:21.910
Let's start at a different node in the
green cycle.

00:04:21.910 --> 00:04:28.110
Also it's not clear what differences does
it make but why shouldn't we try to start

00:04:28.110 --> 00:04:34.900
in a node in the cycle where there is still
unexplored edges.

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:39.300
For example, at this node in the cycle,
there are still unexplored edges

00:04:39.300 --> 00:04:47.560
left, so let's force that to start here,
at this node, and in the beginning, let's

00:04:47.560 --> 00:04:53.790
tell them, first follow the same cycle that
you constructed in the previous step.

00:04:53.790 --> 00:04:57.240
So the ant starts walwking along the green
cycle.

00:04:57.240 --> 00:05:01.460
The ant absolutely doesn't understand why he
has to

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.420
do this kind of thing along the cycle

00:05:04.420 --> 00:05:10.260
that he already constructed but finally he
arrives to the initial

00:05:10.260 --> 00:05:15.600
node and now he realizes that actually now
he can continue walking.

00:05:15.600 --> 00:05:18.130
Because the rest still unexplored edges
from this ?

00:05:18.130 --> 00:05:19.090
node.

00:05:19.090 --> 00:05:24.160
Then continues walking, continues walking,
continues walking, and finally got stuck.

00:05:24.160 --> 00:05:27.850
And of course it got stuck in the same
node where it started.

00:05:27.850 --> 00:05:29.360
What you should do today, 

00:05:29.360 --> 00:05:30.580
afterwards?

00:05:30.580 --> 00:05:32.750
Then afterwards, we should start at

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:35.820
another node with the still unexplored
edges.

00:05:35.820 --> 00:05:37.520
Where is this node?

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:41.930
Can be, well, this is the node, with still
unexplored edges.

00:05:41.930 --> 00:05:45.180
The ant first will be ordered to traverse

00:05:45.180 --> 00:05:47.690
the cycle that has been already
constructed, there's

00:05:47.690 --> 00:05:50.840
this green blue cycle, and then starts,

00:05:50.840 --> 00:05:54.420
continue, continue, continue, the ant is not
happy but

00:05:54.420 --> 00:05:55.550
it's okay.

00:05:55.550 --> 00:06:00.869
Continue and finally, it arrives to the
initial node, but now the

00:06:00.869 --> 00:06:06.140
ant realizes that  the instructions 
are not stupid because there is a possibility

00:06:06.140 --> 00:06:12.270
to explore it further, and we continue,
continue, continue, and finally, the ant

00:06:12.270 --> 00:06:18.740
constructed the cycle that we wanted, that
visits every edge of the graph exactly

00:06:18.740 --> 00:06:19.780
once.

00:06:19.780 --> 00:06:24.020
Not only ant constructed the cycle, we
actually just

00:06:24.020 --> 00:06:28.020
came up with this algorithm for doing this
and this

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:31.570
should accord describe our instructions to
the ant, and you can

00:06:31.570 --> 00:06:34.390
implement it and get real result in an
efficient algorithm.

00:06:35.700 --> 00:06:38.980
And if we would now put an ant 

00:06:38.980 --> 00:06:44.780
to search de Bruijn graphs constructed for
universal string,

00:06:44.780 --> 00:06:47.340
in this case just in three iterations, it
will

00:06:47.340 --> 00:06:51.140
construct Eulerian cycle, and therefore
construct a universal string.

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:55.940
You can check that you will, can, be able
to derive universal string from the cycle.

